## What you got

A playable placeholder: two birds fly through the same pipe field, one
flap button each. You are the yellow one, a bot is the red one, and
whoever clears ten pipes first wins. Space or the up arrow flaps.

It runs at 60 ticks per second through the same realtime session a
networked game uses, and it is deliberately small enough to throw away.

## What to change first

`game/game.go` holds every rule. Replace it a piece at a time:

| Piece | What it decides |
|---|---|
| `State` | the world; everything the session commits |
| `Action` | what one decision is — here, one boolean |
| `Sight` | what one slot may see — narrow this before shipping |
| `RuleSet.Advance` | one simulation step; the physics |
| `RuleSet.Apply` | how a validated action changes the world |
| `RuleSet.Project` | how the world becomes an sight |
| `RuleSet.Evaluate` | the score, progress, and terminal state of a slot |
| `Validator.Legal` | which actions are possible at all |
| `Tuning` | tick rate, send rate, snapshot cadence |

Three things about the generated code are load bearing rather than
incidental:

**No engine import under `game/`.** Rendering and keyboard reading live
in the client entry point, which is the only package allowed to import
Ebitengine. That is what lets the headless targets drop the engine — by
never linking it, not by a build tag. Moving a `vector.DrawFilledRect`
into `game/` breaks that, and the framework's import check is what
catches it.

**No floating point in the simulation.** Every position, velocity, and
constant is `fixmath.F64`. Fixed point is what makes a replay reproduce
on a different machine, and it is the precondition for rollback
netcode. A stray `float64` in `Advance` compiles and then desyncs.

**The RNG lives inside `State`.** The pipe field is a function of the
seed alone, so the same seed replays the same episode. Calling
`rand.Intn` from anywhere would break that quietly.

`Sight` is the piece worth attention early. It is a separate type
from `State` even though this placeholder copies the whole pipe list into
it, because narrowing what a slot can see later is a change to `Project`
alone — and every controller, human or not, reads only its sight.

## Configuration

`ebigent.toml` holds two kinds of setting, and which kind decides when it
takes effect.

`[protocol]` is the game's own contract: how many play, how they connect,
how tightly the loop closes, and who may take which seat. None of it can
differ between your machine and production, so none of it is read at
startup — `ebigent generate` turns it into Go constants and the build
carries them. `ebigent build` runs generate first, so editing the table
and rebuilding is the whole procedure.

`[run]` is read when the process starts, and holds only what two launches
of one binary may legitimately differ on: where it listens, which host it
dials, the clock, and the timing profile. Anything there can be overridden
by an environment variable or a command line option, in that order:

    RUN_TOPOLOGY=dedicated go run ./cmd/{{(index .Targets 0).Name}}
    go run ./cmd/{{(index .Targets 0).Name}} --run-topology dedicated

Under `ebigent`, a configuration option goes before the verb, because the
verb consumes everything after its own name:

    ebigent --run-topology dedicated build {{(index .Targets 0).Name}}

The timing profile is the one run setting every peer of a match has to
agree on. A client reconstructs state against the profile the host runs,
so two machines with different tick rates do not negotiate — they
desynchronize. Set it per deployment, not per machine.

## Recording, and what it is for

A corpus cannot be collected retroactively, so the pipeline is wired
before there is any data to put through it. `cmd/simulation` records
every decision every seat made, with the sight it was made from:

    go run ./cmd/simulation      # writes corpus/{{.Name}}-sim-0001/
    ebigent distill              # mines it into behavior/chips.json

`ebigent distill` spawns `{{.DistillEntry}}`, which is a program in this
project rather than something inside the toolchain. It has to be: a rule
about this game is a Go function over a `game.Sight`, and a compiled
`ebigent` has no way to receive one from a module it was built without.

`Vocabulary` in that file is the part to write. It ships with two
judgements about the placeholder bird, which is enough for the first run
to find something and not enough for anything else — a rule that names a
fact about the position generalizes, and a rule that names a coordinate
memorizes.

Nothing is generated until you approve it. Distillation reproduces logged
play, and logged play includes mistakes, so the mined chips wait in
`behavior/chips.json` with `"approved": false` until you say otherwise.
Approve some and run `ebigent distill` again to get `behavior/gen` — an
ordinary `session.Agent`, in Go you can read and diff.
